# Target C-II — unloaded strengthening of global outer-walk penetration

**Date:** 2026-09-15  
**Repository base:** `687b6ddf5b2ecb2514928a7d505674e3d2fceacc` plus the independently hostile-audited loaded-route exclusion `80561aa` / `ca938ae0`  
**Status:** proved proof-only composition; theorem-state authority unchanged  
**Scope:** fully force-visible, no-container/full-core Type-II low-`t` branch  
**LEVEL I STATUS:** `STILL OPEN`

The global straight-event theorem `1283dfb` obtained a large population of strongly
transverse physical contacts from the entire distinguished outer walk. Its finite
count retained a degree-two internal-route escape and paid for that escape through
outer bridges.

The independently proved loaded-route theorem `80561aa`, hostile-audited in
`ca938ae0`, removes that escape completely: every physical vertex occurring internally
on a Section-63 distinguished route is unloaded. Canonical C26 therefore gives contact
degree at least three at every such occurrence.

As a result the bridge term disappears from the global straight-event theorem. At the
current proof-only low-`t` endpoint this raises the physical-contact floor from
`0.6388257253 D` to

\[
\boxed{0.6402677128496573613\ldots D-o(D)}.
\]

Every counted contact has absolute transverse component strictly greater than `1/2`
relative to the loaded-hull chord direction associated with the straight occurrence
that selected it.

## 1. Inputs

Let

\[
W_i:b_i\leadsto b_{i+1}
\]

be the Section-63 distinguished outer-face contact routes between consecutive
positively loaded force-hull vertices. Put

\[
s_i=|b_i-b_{i+1}|,
\qquad
u_i={b_{i+1}-b_i\over s_i},
\]

and orient every unit edge occurrence of `W_i` from `b_i` to `b_{i+1}`.
If `theta_{i,j}` is the angle from `u_i` to the `j`th oriented unit edge occurrence,
the exact directional-energy identity is

\[
\boxed{
d_{out}:=b-\operatorname{per}(F)
=\sum_i\sum_j(1-\cos\theta_{i,j}).
}
\tag{US.1}
\]

The total number of internal distinguished-route occurrences is exactly

\[
\boxed{
\sum_i(\ell_i-1)=b-q=:x_B.
}
\tag{US.2}
\]

Both identities are occurrence-counted and allow bridges and repeated outer-face
vertices.

## 2. Directional concentration on the entire outer walk

Fix

\[
0<\beta\le\pi/6,
\qquad
c_\beta:=1-\cos\beta.
\]

Call an outer-route edge occurrence bad when `|theta_{i,j}|>=beta`. Equation (US.1)
gives

\[
A_\beta\le {d_{out}\over c_\beta}.
\tag{US.3}
\]

Call an internal route occurrence `beta`-straight when both adjacent oriented route
edges are good. One bad edge occurrence is adjacent to at most two internal
occurrences, so the number `S_beta` of straight internal occurrences satisfies

\[
\boxed{
S_\beta
\ge x_B-{2d_{out}\over c_\beta}.
}
\tag{US.4}
\]

No tooth/long-side split is used.

## 3. Every internal straight occurrence is branching

The proved loaded-route exclusion `80561aa` uses the actual Section-63 occurrence
partition and the selected-stress loaded-nonarticulation theorem. If a positively
loaded physical vertex occurred internally on one `W_i`, that occurrence would be
non-distinguished, so the same physical vertex would occur at least twice on the full
outer-face walk. A repeated facial vertex is an articulation vertex. The selected
stress proves that positively loaded vertices are not articulations. Hence

\[
\boxed{
\text{every physical vertex occurring internally on every }W_i\text{ is unloaded.}
}
\tag{US.5}
\]

The hostile audit `ca938ae0` independently rechecks both the facial-repetition lemma
and the loaded-nonarticulation argument and passes this implication at proof-only fully
force-visible C-II scope.

Canonical C26 then gives

\[
\boxed{3\le\deg_C(x)\le5}
\tag{US.6}
\]

for every internal route vertex `x`. In particular every straight occurrence has at
least one contact edge not used by its predecessor/successor route pair.

## 4. Strong transverse direction at every straight occurrence

At a `beta`-straight occurrence on `W_i`, viewed from its physical vertex `x`, the ray
to the predecessor lies within `beta` of `-u_i`, while the ray to the successor lies
within `beta` of `+u_i`.

Every other contact ray at `x` is separated from both route rays by strictly more than
`pi/3`; equality would create a unit equilateral triangle. Therefore each extra unit
contact `xw` satisfies

\[
\boxed{
|(w-x)\cdot Ju_i|
>\sin(\pi/3-\beta).
}
\tag{US.7}
\]

At `beta=pi/6`, every extra contact has absolute transverse component strictly greater
than `1/2`.

## 5. At most two straight occurrences per physical vertex

The two route-contact rays at a `beta`-straight occurrence have smaller angular
separation strictly greater than

\[
\pi-2\beta.
\]

They delimit one angular sector occupied by the unbounded face. Either sector between
the two rays has angular measure at least the smaller separation, so every straight
occurrence consumes an outer-face sector of size strictly greater than

\[
\pi-2\beta.
\tag{US.8}
\]

Distinct outer-face occurrences of one physical vertex correspond to disjoint sectors
in the cyclic rotation at that vertex. If `beta<=pi/6`, three such sectors would have
total angular measure strictly greater than `2pi`. Therefore one physical vertex
supports at most two straight occurrences.

## 6. Inject one selected contact incidence per straight occurrence

Fix one physical vertex `x` supporting `r` straight occurrences. By the previous
section, `r<=2`.

For an occurrence `o`, let `P_o` be its unordered predecessor/successor physical-edge
pair and let

\[
A_o:=E_C(x)\setminus P_o.
\]

By (US.6), every `A_o` is nonempty. Every edge in `A_o` obeys the transverse bound
(US.7).

If `r=1`, choose any member of `A_o`. If `r=2`, the two outer-face sectors are
distinct, hence `P_1!=P_2`.

- If `deg_C(x)=3`, each `A_o` is the singleton complement of `P_o`, so the two
  allowed singletons are distinct.
- If `deg_C(x)>=4`, both allowed sets have size at least two, and Hall's condition for
  two indices is automatic.

Thus the straight occurrences at `x` admit distinct representative contact-edge
incidences. Applying this independently at every physical vertex selects exactly one
edge incidence per straight occurrence.

One undirected physical contact edge has only two endpoints. Local injectivity means
it is selected at most once at each endpoint. Therefore, if `E_perp(beta)` is the
number of distinct physical selected edges,

\[
\boxed{
2E_\perp(\beta)\ge S_\beta.
}
\tag{US.9}
\]

Combining (US.4) and (US.9) gives the finite global theorem

\[
\boxed{
2E_\perp(\beta)
\ge
x_B-{2d_{out}\over1-\cos\beta}
\qquad(0<\beta\le\pi/6).
}
\tag{US.10}
\]

Unlike `1283dfb`, no outer-bridge or bridge-isoperimetry term remains.

## 7. Type-II normalization

On a convergent full-core Type-II subsequence write

\[
t={q-k\over D},
\qquad
\kappa={k\over D},
\qquad
\delta={d_{out}\over D}.
\]

Near-disk loaded-hull perimeter convergence gives

\[
{x_B\over D}
=\pi+\delta-t-\kappa+o(1).
\tag{US.11}
\]

Dividing (US.10) by `D` gives

\[
\boxed{
\liminf {E_\perp(\beta)\over D}
\ge
{1\over2}
\left[
\pi-t-\kappa
-\left({2\over1-\cos\beta}-1\right)\delta
\right].
}
\tag{US.12}
\]

## 8. Joint optimization with the low-`t` network squeeze

Set

\[
\beta={\pi\over6},
\qquad
c_*:=1-{\sqrt3\over2}.
\]

Proof-only AG42 plus audited Section 171 gives

\[
2\delta+{\kappa\over2}\le R(t),
\qquad
R(t):={9t\over2}-2\pi-C_0,
\]

where

\[
C_0=2\sqrt{\Delta_2}+0.00042,
\qquad
\Delta_2={\pi\over2\sqrt3}.
\]

To minimize the right side of (US.12), maximize

\[
\kappa+\left({2\over c_*}-1\right)\delta
\]

under the network constraint. Spending one unit of the network budget on `kappa`
yields objective gain `2`, while spending one unit on `delta` yields

\[
{1\over2}\left({2\over c_*}-1\right)
={1\over c_*}-{1\over2}
>2.
\]

Thus the worst point is

\[
\boxed{
\delta={R(t)\over2},
\qquad
\kappa=0.
}
\tag{US.13}
\]

and every survivor in the low-`t` scope satisfies

\[
\boxed{
\liminf {E_\perp\over D}
\ge
{1\over2}
\left[
\pi-t-
\left({1\over1-\sqrt3/2}-{1\over2}\right)R(t)
\right].
}
\tag{US.14}
\]

## 9. Current endpoint

At

\[
t_{42}=C_{146}+0.0013751
=1.8208886379794447041\ldots,
\]

one has

\[
R(t_{42})=0.00576795,
\qquad
\delta_{worst}=0.002883975,
\qquad
\kappa_{worst}=0.
\]

Then

\[
{x_B\over D}
\ge1.3235879906103485343\ldots-o(1),
\]

\[
{A_{\pi/6}\over D}
\le0.0215262824555169058\ldots+o(1),
\]

and

\[
{S_{\pi/6}\over D}
\ge1.2805354256993147228\ldots-o(1).
\]

Equation (US.9) therefore gives

\[
\boxed{
{E_\perp\over D}
\ge0.64026771284965736138\ldots-o(1).
}
\tag{US.15}
\]

Every selected edge has absolute transverse component strictly greater than `1/2`.

## 10. Frontier effect

The current low-`t` Type-II endpoint is now forced into the following source-level
normal form:

```text
x_B >= 1.3235879906 D-o(D) internal distinguished-route occurrences
+ d_out <= 0.002883975 D+o(D)
 -> S_pi/6 >= 1.2805354257 D-o(D) near-straight internal occurrences
 -> every such occurrence is unloaded and degree >=3
 -> occurrence-injective transverse selection
 -> E_perp >= 0.6402677128 D-o(D) distinct physical contacts
 -> every selected contact has transverse component >1/2.
```

This supersedes bridge-paid straight-event counting and is stronger than the complete
tooth/long-side physicalization chain. The tooth and long-side theorems remain useful
for finer geometry but are no longer needed to obtain a large physical contact
population from the low-`t` endpoint.

C-II remains open. The unresolved step is global rather than local: explain how more
than `0.640D` robust transverse contacts can be routed through the sparse pentagon /
strict-rhombus stopped-train network while the same source supports the near-extremal
Woodall diameter graph. Vanishing-shear rhombic strips show that no fixed local
rhombus-area penalty can supply this contradiction.

The next useful theorem should control train occupancy or diameter-brace reuse for the
straight occurrences counted in (US.4), or turn the resulting boundary structure into
a finite source-preserving deformation.

## 11. Reproducibility

Run

```bash
python3 research/verify_target_c_global_outer_straight_unloaded_strengthening.py
```

for the high-precision constant checks.
